<!-- ENGINEERING PHILOSOPHY -->

<section class="section">

<div class="container">

<p class="section-label">ENGINEERING APPROACH</p>

<h2>Follow the signal.</h2>

<p class="section-intro">
    Whether the problem involves an electronic circuit, a TCP
    connection, an authentication token, a Kubernetes workload,
    a security event or an application failure, my troubleshooting
    process follows the same basic principles.
</p>


<div class="method-grid">

<div class="method-card">
<span>01</span>
<h3>Understand</h3>
<p>
Understand the architecture and the relationships between
the components involved.
</p>
</div>


<div class="method-card">
<span>02</span>
<h3>Establish</h3>
<p>
Determine what normal or expected behavior should look like.
</p>
</div>


<div class="method-card">
<span>03</span>
<h3>Observe</h3>
<p>
Gather logs, packets, telemetry, system state and other
evidence before drawing conclusions.
</p>
</div>


<div class="method-card">
<span>04</span>
<h3>Trace</h3>
<p>
Follow the signal path through the systems involved and
identify where behavior changes.
</p>
</div>


<div class="method-card">
<span>05</span>
<h3>Isolate</h3>
<p>
Reduce the problem until the responsible component,
configuration or behavior can be identified.
</p>
</div>


<div class="method-card">
<span>06</span>
<h3>Verify</h3>
<p>
Correct the issue, validate the result and preserve
what was learned for the next investigation.
</p>
</div>

</div>

</div>

</section>


<!-- LEADERSHIP + ENGINEERING -->

